            <ArticleTitle>Osteomyelitis and Discitis Following Translumbar Repair of a Type II Endoleak.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>458-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Here we present the case of an 80-year-old man who developed a type II endoleak following endovascular abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. Initial attempts at treating the endoleak via a transarterial approach were unsuccessful; therefore the patient underwent percutaneous translumbar endoleak embolization. Approximately 1 month following the translumbar procedure, he developed back pain, with subsequent workup revealing osteomyelitis and discitis as a complication following repair via the translumbar approach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
